First Author: Brahmbhatt
Title: The lung allocation score and other available models lack predictive accuracy for post-lung transplant survival
Source number: 6  ￼

Very short summary: This retrospective UNOS study evaluated whether the LAS and several alternative models could accurately predict post-lung-transplant mortality. Five key findings were: (1) 19,900 adults undergoing first lung transplant between 2005 and 2017 were studied; (2) all tested models had poor discrimination for 1-year death or re-transplant, with AUCs only 0.55 to 0.62; (3) all models had reasonable negative predictive value but poor positive predictive value, with all PPVs below 0.25; (4) the LAS post-transplant survival measure consistently overestimated mortality risk, especially in higher deciles and at 3-, 6-, 9-, and 12-month time points; and (5) machine-learning models, disease-specific models, donor-variable models, and 3-year models did not improve prediction. Tables and figures used directly here include Table 1, Table 2, Table 3, Figure 1, Figure 2, Figure 3, Figure 4, and Figure 5.  ￼  ￼  ￼

