Section 3: Intervention & AI Methods
	•	Primary ML Component: Yes
	•	Study Design: Retrospective Cohort
	•	AI Model Architecture: Random Forest
	•	Algorithm Name: LASSO and Random Forests; compared with LAS, Chan et al. model, and a clinician model
	•	Input Variables (Data Modality): Tabular (EMR/Clinical data), Donor metrics
	•	Comparator / Standard of Care: Linear Risk Score (e.g., LAS, EuroSCORE)
	•	Validation Method: Internal Split (Train/Test)
	•	Explainability / Interpretability Used: No
	•	Feature Selection Method: Manual/Clinical expertise
	•	Hyperparameter Tuning Reported: Not Reported
	•	Section 3 Comments/Quotes: This was a retrospective cohort study comparing five models: LAS, Chan et al., a clinician logistic model with 27 a priori covariates, LASSO, and Random Forests. Because the paper centers on evaluating machine-learning approaches alongside existing risk scores, the primary ML component is Yes. The machine-learning architectures explicitly reported were LASSO and Random Forests. Inputs were pre-transplant recipient covariates from UNOS, with donor covariates added in secondary analyses. The main comparators were LAS and Chan’s risk score, both best categorized here as linear risk-score comparators. The validation approach used an 85% development set and 15% validation set. No SHAP/LIME-type interpretability or hyperparameter-tuning details were reported in the retrieved text.  ￼  ￼

Section 4: AI Quality & Reproducibility (CONVINCE Standards)
	•	Missing Data Handling: Simple Imputation (Mean/Median)
	•	Class Imbalance Addressed: No
	•	Code Availability: No
	•	Data Preprocessing / Normalization Described: Yes
	•	Training Size (N): 16915
	•	Test Size (N): 2985
	•	Section 4 Comments/Quotes: The paper states that “for binary and categorical variables, patients with missing measurements were grouped into their own category” and “missing measurements for continuous variables were estimated using mean imputation,” so this is best classified as simple imputation. I found no mention of resampling, weighting, or other imbalance handling. Preprocessing/model-building details were described, including a priori variable selection for the clinician model and alternative ML approaches. Code availability was not reported. The development/validation split was 85%/15%, which corresponds to approximately 16,915 training and 2,985 validation patients from 19,900 total.  ￼

